Notes on EVRC-WB executable compilation
---------------------------------------
on Unix  - use Makefile
on Cygwin - use Makefile.cyg

Notes on EVRC-WB executable usage
---------------------------------

Encoder usage:
	Evrc_wb -i input_speech_file -o encoded_packet_file -X OP=<0 or 1 or 2 or 3 or Music or Music_MDCT> -X Fsinp=<8000 or 16000> -e [-D]

Decoder usage:
	EvrcB -i encoded_packet_file -o decoded_output_speech_file -X Fsop=<8000 or 16000> -d [-D]


Notes:

The -X OP=0 {or 1 or 2 or 3 or Music or Music_MDCT} exercises the following sample operating points of the EVRC-WB encoder.
When OP=0, EVRC-WB tries to match the active speech average channel rate of 9.3 kbps
When OP=1, EVRC-WB tries to match the active speech average channel rate of 6.6 kbps
When OP=2, EVRC-WB tries to match the active speech average channel rate of 5.8 kbps
When OP=3, EVRC-WB encodes wideband input speech sampled at 16000 samples/sec using one of CELP/NELP/Silence coding modes on a frame-by-frame basis at the active speech maximum channel rate of 9.6 kbps.
When OP=Music, EVRC-WB encodes wideband input signal sampled at 16000 samples/sec using one of MDCT/RCELP/NELP/Silence coding modes on a frame-by-frame basis at the active speech maximum channel rate of 9.6 kbps.
When OP=Music_MDCT, EVRC-WB encodes wideband input signal sampled at 16000 samples/sec using one of MDCT/NELP/Silence coding modes on a frame by frame basis at the active speech maximum channel rate of 9.6 kbps.

The -X Fsinp= 16000{ or 8000} is used to specify the sampling frequency of the input signal. The table below describes the allowed input signal sampling freqencies for each sample operating point

==================================================================================================================
Fsinp= |  OP=0         |  OP=1         |  OP=2         |  OP=3       |  OP=Music     |  OP=Music_MDCT
==================================================================================================================
8000   |  Allowed      |  Allowed      |  Allowed      |  NotAllowed |  Not Allowed  |  Not Allowed
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
16000  |  Not Allowed  |  Not Allowed  |  Not Allowed  |  Allowed    |  Allowed      |  Allowed      
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The -X Fsop= 16000{ or 8000} is used to specify the sampling frequency of the decoded output signal. The table below describes the allowed output signal sampling freqencies when the input packet was encoded in different sample operating point

==================================================================================================================
Fsop=  |  OP=0          |  OP=1         |  OP=2         |  OP=3                 |  OP=Music     |  OP=Music_MDCT
==================================================================================================================
8000   |  Allowed       |  Allowed      |  Allowed      |  NotAllowed           |  Not Allowed  |  Not Allowed
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
16000  |  Not Allowed   |  Not Allowed  |  Not Allowed  |    Allowed            |  Allowed      |  Allowed      
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Note: The above limitations in the modes that are "Not allowed" are only temporary, and during EVRC-WB software Ballot resolution and V&V, the above Not Allowed modes will be enabled.

-D option is used to enable DTX at the encoder  and decoder



Input/Output Data formats
- input_speech_file, and decoded_output_speech_file 
	- 8 or 16 KHz, 16-bit(native format (little or big endian)) per sample 
- encoded_packet_file 
	- 12 16-bit words per frame 
	- 1st word is the rate word (4-full, 3-half, 2-quarter, 1-eighth, 0-blank,0xE-erasure)
	- followed by 11 words of data (regardless of rate)


Notes on compiling a VoIP client simulation to interface to NCIM
----------------------------------------------------------------

Evrc_wb.a 
----------
EVRC-WB VoIP client (DeJitter buffer and Decoder) library that interfaces to NCIM in
3GPP2 C.P1009 (3GPP2 Multimedia Services Evaluation Methodology: Software Tools).

API to this library in codec.h

